   <p>The third is overstated aggregate group value. When the same underlying promoter capital appears, directly or through cross-shareholding, as equity in multiple listed entities, a naive sum-of-the-parts view of "the group's" market capitalisation double-counts capital that exists only once in economic reality. A promoter family whose disclosed net worth appears, on paper, to span a bank, an insurer, and three hydropower companies may in substance be leveraging one core pool of capital across all five balance sheets simultaneously, with each entity's reported strength partly dependent on the others continuing to perform. This is not necessarily fraudulent — it can be entirely disclosed and entirely legal — but it means that the intuitive investor habit of treating "a strong group" as a blanket credit-positive for every entity bearing its name is analytically unsound.</p>
