   <p>Underneath all three ratios sits EPS, and underneath EPS sits the weighted-average-share mechanics that Nepal's recurring bonus-share culture makes unavoidable: rights shares dilute prospectively from their issue date, while bonus shares — because they represent no new capital — must be applied retroactively, restating prior-year comparatives so that reported earnings growth is real growth rather than a denominator artifact. The 2015-era NRB capital directive that triggered years of sector-wide bonus issuances across Nepali banks is the clearest historical illustration of why this restatement discipline matters for anyone doing multi-year comparisons.</p>
