   <h2>Lesson 46.1 — The Logic of Discounting: Why a Rupee Tomorrow Is Worth Less Than a Rupee Today</h2>
   <p>Every DCF valuation rests on one simple, almost obvious human intuition: a promise of money in the future is worth less than the same amount of money in hand right now. If a friend offers you a choice between NPR 100,000 today or NPR 100,000 in exactly one year, and you trust the friend completely, you would almost certainly take the money today. Why? Because you could deposit that NPR 100,000 in a fixed deposit at a commercial bank, earn interest on it for a year, and end up with more than NPR 100,000 by the time your friend's promised payment would have arrived. Money today can be put to work; money promised for later cannot be put to work until it arrives. This is the entire idea behind what economists and analysts call the time value of money — the principle that a given sum of money is worth more the sooner it is received, because of its earning potential in the interim.</p>
