   <h2>The Kaskikot Circuit</h2>
   <p>Sunita Rana had done everything Part XI told her to do, or so she believed. She had read the chapters on liquidity as a first-order risk, she could recite the ADV rule from memory, and she understood, in the abstract, why free-float allocation limits mattered more than price-earnings ratios for a certain class of NEPSE counters. What she had not done, in October of the previous year, was translate any of that theory into an order she actually typed into her broker's TMS terminal. She had a target: build a position of 60,000 shares in Mustang Resorts Ltd, a thinly traded tourism-and-hospitality counter trading at roughly NPR 480, with a listed base of 40 lakh shares of which barely 12 lakh moved freely outside promoter lock-in. She had a thesis: post-pandemic tourism recovery, a coming dividend announcement, a technical breakout. What she did not have was a plan for how to get in, and, more dangerously, no plan at all for how she would get out.</p>
