   <p>This chapter taught you to build your own personal regulatory tracker for NEPSE investing, distinct from any external resource — a structured, repeatable system rather than a vague habit of reading the news. You learned to distinguish Nepal Rastra Bank, which controls system-wide liquidity through tools like the CAR floor, CD ratio ceiling, CRR, and SLR, from the Securities Board of Nepal, which regulates the market's own conduct through margin lending caps and disclosure rules — and why these two sources typically call for different kinds of reactions, one slow and system-wide, the other sometimes sudden and sharply timed. You built a five-column log — Date Noticed, Source, Change Summary, Sectors or Companies Affected, Action Taken — and walked through Damber Prasad Sharma's worked example of a CAR floor revision, from first notice through differentiated action across three affected holdings. You learned a three-level severity scale, Watch, Act, and Urgent, to separate genuine mechanism from noise, and a two-part review rhythm — a light weekly scan and a deeper quarterly reckoning — that turns a pile of logged entries into an honest, improving record of your own judgment over time.</p>
