   <p>Every credit expansion cycle in Nepal has ended the same way: NPL ratios rise, provisioning charges increase, net profit falls, and banking stocks reprice downward. This is not a prediction — it is a documented pattern that has repeated across every major cycle in Nepal's banking history.</p>
   <p>Understanding the mechanism of the phase-shift from expansion to contraction is as important as understanding the expansion itself, because the contraction phase is where permanent capital losses are made.</p>
