   <p>Broker Shareholding: The Intermediary Interest</p>
   <p>Licensed stockbrokers hold approximately 17% of NEPSE collectively. This creates an unusual alignment: the intermediaries whose income depends on trading volume — commissions are charged per transaction — are also partial owners of the exchange that facilitates those transactions. High turnover benefits brokers on two levels simultaneously: as commission earners and as equity holders in a more profitable exchange.</p>
   <p>For the investor, this matters because it means that NEPSE's institutional structure is not neutral on the question of trading frequency. There are no broker-owners with a financial interest in client buy-and-hold strategies. There are no broker-owners who benefit from clients making fewer, more deliberate transactions. The incentive structure, embedded in the ownership architecture, tilts toward activity. This is relevant when evaluating broker advice, when assessing whether margin lending is pitched too aggressively, and when considering why NEPSE's trading volume can sometimes spike on rumour alone.</p>
