   <h3>Using Market Cap Intelligently</h3>
   <p>None of this means market cap is useless. For comparative purposes — comparing the relative size of two listed companies, tracking how the total value of the financial sector has changed over time, or identifying when market exuberance has pushed aggregate valuations far above reasonable earnings multiples — market cap remains a powerful tool. The discipline lies in knowing what it measures and what it does not.</p>
   <p>A NEPSE market cap-to-GDP ratio above 80% should prompt questions about valuation, liquidity conditions, and the cyclical position of the banking sector. A ratio below 40% may signal undervaluation — or simply the end of a liquidity cycle. Context, not the number alone, determines the interpretation.</p>
