   <p>Sub-Brokers: The Informal Extension</p>
   <p>Beyond the 50 licensed brokers, a network of informal sub-brokers operates in Nepal — individuals who facilitate investor access to licensed brokers, often in geographic areas where licensed brokers have no presence. Sub-brokers collect orders from investors, route them through a licensed broker's account, and earn a share of the commission. Their operation exists in a regulatory grey zone: SEBON has regulations for formal sub-broker registration, but many informal participants operate without registration.</p>
   <p>For investors using sub-broker networks — particularly in provincial areas — the risks are real: the sub-broker has no regulatory accountability for order execution quality, client fund safety, or advice accuracy. Cases of sub-broker fraud — collecting investor funds without routing them to the market, or diverting client accounts — have occurred and received limited regulatory redress. Investors should, where at all possible, establish direct relationships with licensed brokers through digital platforms rather than operating through informal sub-broker channels.</p>
