   <p>The most common overreaction readers have to a stale figure is treating it as evidence that the whole book has failed them. This reaction is understandable — if you catch one number wrong, it is natural to wonder what else might be wrong — but it confuses two very different situations, and telling them apart is one of the more important judgment calls this chapter can teach.</p>
   <p>A number becoming stale means the world changed after the number was written down, and the number needs to be brought up to date. This is expected, routine, and says nothing negative about the quality of the reasoning that used the number. If NRB raises the CD ratio ceiling from one level to a slightly higher one, the Canon Score's capital strength category does not need to be redesigned — it needs the ceiling figure updated, and every bank's score recalculated against the new ceiling. The category still measures the right thing; it is measuring it against a new bar.</p>
