   <h3>The Broker as Intermediary and as Principal</h3>
   <p>In a well-functioning market, a broker acts as your agent. Their job is to take your order and execute it in a manner that is most favourable to you — getting you the best price, the best fill rate, and the fastest execution. This is called best execution, and regulators in advanced markets require brokers to demonstrate it.</p>
   <p>In NEPSE, brokers play a dual role. They are agents for retail clients, yes. But many are also principals — they trade for their own accounts. This creates a fundamental conflict of interest that is rarely disclosed clearly to retail clients and even less rarely resolved in the retail client's favour.</p>
