   <h3>The Defining Anomaly of Closed-Ended Funds</h3>
   <p>No phenomenon in mutual fund investing is as simultaneously intuitive and puzzling as the persistent discount at which closed-ended funds trade relative to their Net Asset Value. In a perfectly efficient market, a fund holding Rs. 100 worth of assets per unit should trade at Rs. 100. Yet in Nepal — as in markets globally — closed-ended funds routinely trade at Rs. 80, Rs. 85, or even Rs. 70 per unit when the NAV is Rs. 100. This discount is the central intellectual puzzle of closed-ended fund investing.</p>
