   <p>If free cash flow is the numerator of a DCF, the discount rate is the denominator, and in a market like Nepal's, the denominator is where most of the genuine difficulty — and most of the genuine judgment — lives. The standard tool for estimating the cost of equity (the annual return shareholders require to compensate them for the risk of owning a particular stock, as opposed to the cost of debt, which is simply the interest rate lenders charge) is the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM). CAPM states:</p>
   <p>Cost of Equity = Risk-Free Rate + Beta × Equity Risk Premium</p>
   <p>Each of these three pieces requires real judgment when applied to a NEPSE-listed company, so each is worth walking through carefully.</p>
