   <p>No analyst can credibly forecast a company's cash flows line by line, year by year, forever. Most DCF models therefore build an explicit forecast for a limited number of years — commonly five, sometimes as many as ten for a business still ramping toward a stable, mature state, such as a hydropower plant in its early years of operation — and then collapse everything the business is expected to generate beyond that horizon into a single figure called terminal value, calculated as of the last year of the explicit forecast and then discounted back to today just like any other cash flow.</p>
