   <p>The next chapter, Chapter 57, "Free Float-Based Allocation Limits," extends this liquidity-engineering framework by examining a variable that sits upstream of ADV and helps explain why it varies so much from scrip to scrip in the first place: free float, the portion of a company's total shares that is actually available for public trading rather than locked up in promoter holdings, government stakes, or other long-term, non-trading blocks. Where this chapter built a rule around how much of a stock trades on an average day, Chapter 57 builds a complementary rule around how much of a stock's total capital structure is even eligible to trade at all — and shows how a narrow free float can quietly cap a position's liquidity ceiling even in a company whose total market capitalisation looks large enough to seem perfectly safe.</p>
