-  <h2>Lesson 66.3 — Score Deterioration as a Sell and Review Trigger</h2>
+  <h2 id="lesson-66-3-score-deterioration-as-a-sell-and-review-trigger">Lesson 66.3 — Score Deterioration as a Sell and Review Trigger</h2>
   <p>A patient with a chronic condition does not wait for a doctor's advice only when she feels sick. She gets a blood test every few months, even when she feels fine, because the numbers can move before the symptoms do. If her cholesterol or blood sugar jumps sharply between two routine tests, a good doctor does not necessarily prescribe a drastic change immediately — but she does insist on a proper follow-up: repeat the test, ask what changed in diet or medication, rule out a lab error, and only then decide on a course of action. The jump itself is not the diagnosis. It is the trigger that forces a proper look.</p>
   <p>This is the model for using score deterioration in your portfolio. A deterioration trigger is a preset drop in a held company's Canon Score, measured between two re-scoring dates, that forces you to open a formal review — not a rule that forces you to sell automatically. The distinction matters enormously. Chapter 61 built a review discipline precisely so that difficult decisions get made with a clear head, on a schedule, rather than in a panic or, just as dangerously, through inertia and denial. Score deterioration is one of the cleanest, most objective ways to trip that review discipline into action.</p>
   <p>A workable trigger rule, calibrated to the same rubric from Chapter 64, has two legs:</p>
