     An investor buying into a hydropower company ahead of the monsoon season — when river-fed plants generate more electricity and revenue typically rises — might write: "Bought at NPR 310 because Q4 generation figures beat my expectation, PPA (power purchase agreement) terms with the utility are unchanged, and Canon Score rose to 74 this quarter mainly on improved cash flow. Expect the dry-season quarter to look weaker — that is normal for this sector and not itself a reason to sell. Would reconsider only if generation falls short of the prior dry season on a like-for-like basis, or if there is a change to the PPA tariff." Eight months later, whether the stock is up or down, this investor can reread exactly what they believed and check it against what actually happened — not against a hazy, outcome-coloured memory of what they believed.
