   </div>
   <p>This last row deserves emphasis. Once a year, for every single holding, Anjana forces herself to answer one blunt question: if I did not already own this, would I buy it today, at today's price, knowing everything I now know? This is sometimes called a "fresh eyes" test, and it exists specifically to counter a well-documented human bias — the tendency to keep holding something simply because you already hold it, a pattern investing psychology calls the endowment effect, where owning something makes us value it more than we would if we were considering it as a new purchase. The annual routine, done honestly, cuts through that bias once a year, even when the weekly and monthly routines, focused on incremental change, might not have forced the question directly.</p>
